            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"An event, miracle, or object that points beyond itself to authenticate a divine messenger, reveal God's glory, or validate a divine message, most prominently used in the Gospel of John to describe Jesus' miracles as proofs of his divine identity.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"John 2:11\",\n    \"John 20:30-31\",\n    \"Isaiah 7:14\",\n    \"Exodus 4:8\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"A physical object, practice, or phenomenon serving as a visible marker and permanent reminder of a covenant between God and humanity.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Genesis 9:12-13\",\n        \"Genesis 17:11\",\n        \"Exodus 31:13\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"dominant\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"An eschatological omen or cosmic anomaly indicating the end of the age, the return of Christ, or impending divine judgment.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Matthew 24:3\",\n        \"Luke 21:25\",\n        \"Revelation 12:1\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"A theological or sacramental symbol functioning as a visible expression of an invisible spiritual reality or grace.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Romans 4:11\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"minority\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": true,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"In biblical theology, the concept of a sign (Hebrew 'ot, Greek semeion) is rarely just a wonder or an end in itself; it functions semiotically to direct the observer toward faith, a covenantal promise, or a specific theological truth.\"\n}",
